description:
Found by s507d (the pcl documentation batch) while checking the claim "-c runs BEGIN blocks and use as perl -c does".  Probed against perl 5.40.3 on main 50dbd195 (= 08de9e4f + records).

Reproducer 1 (the mechanism):
  perl -e 'CHECK { print "chk
" } use List::Util; BEGIN { print "b
" }'   -> b / chk
  pcl  -e 'CHECK { print "chk
" } use List::Util; BEGIN { print "b
" }'   -> chk / b
  Same with `use POSIX ();` and `use File::Basename;`.  With no `use`, or only pragmas (`use strict;`), the order is right.

Reproducer 2 (what a user sees, `pcl -c`):
  printf 'use strict;
use warnings;
use File::Basename;
use NoSuchModuleXyz;
print 1;
' > c9.pl
  perl -c c9.pl  -> Can't locate NoSuchModuleXyz.pm ... BEGIN failed--compilation aborted at c9.pl line 4.  exit 2
  pcl  -c c9.pl  -> c9.pl syntax OK  exit 0
  printf 'use List::Util qw(sum);
BEGIN { die "begin died
" }
print sum(1,2), "
";
' > c7.pl
  perl -c c7.pl  -> begin died / BEGIN failed  exit 2;   pcl -c c7.pl -> syntax OK, exit 0
  printf 'use List::Util qw(sum);
BEGIN { print STDOUT "b
" }
print 3, "
";
' > c2.pl
  perl -c c2.pl  -> b on stdout;  pcl -c c2.pl -> nothing on stdout

`pcl -c` is PCLSwitches::expand_program's `CHECK { print STDERR "NAME syntax OK
"; close STDOUT; require POSIX; POSIX::_exit(0) }` put in front of the program; that is right only if CHECK runs after the whole compile phase.  The phase model across sections (the first non-pragma `use` starts a new section?) runs the CHECK at the end of the FIRST section.  Fix belongs to the phase model, not to -c.  docs/pcl-commands.md's "where pcl differs from perl" table names this until it is fixed.
